# Supabase project "Purview US East" (org: PurviewOps, ref mpwhtwflbehvmhtcssdn). The
# intake route writes the raw payload to pv_intake_raw with the service role
# key, so this file is server side only. Neither var is NEXT_PUBLIC_ and
# neither should become one.
#
# The service role key is the one under Project Settings > API Keys > secret.
# Not the publishable / anon key: pv_intake_raw has RLS on with no policies, so
# anon is refused and the write fails.
SUPABASE_URL=https://mpwhtwflbehvmhtcssdn.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Site assistant -------------------------------------------------------
# Where app/api/chat/route.ts forwards a chat turn after it has written the
# user turn to pv_chat_log. n8n owns the system prompt, the model call, the
# trigger, and the write of the assistant turn, per purview_assistant_spec.md.
#
# Server side only, and deliberately not a constant in app/chat.ts: that file
# is imported by the widget, so anything in it ships to the browser.
#
# Leave it unset and the route logs that it has no answer to give and returns
# a failure. The question is still written to pv_chat_log first, which is the
# whole point of the ordering.
#
# The widget itself is off until CHAT_WEBHOOK_URL in app/chat.ts is set to
# '/api/chat'. Set both or neither.
N8N_CHAT_WEBHOOK_URL=

# --- HubSpot schema (ops only) --------------------------------------------
# Private App token for scripts/hubspot-schema.mjs, which builds and verifies
# the section 3 schema: the custom contact and deal properties and the Audit
# pipeline. Nothing in app/ reads this. It is an ops credential, not a runtime
# one, so it must never become NEXT_PUBLIC_ and never ship to the browser.
#
# The MCP HubSpot connector cannot do this work. It creates records against
# properties that already exist; it has no tool that makes a property, a
# pipeline, or a stage. Hence a token and the REST API.
#
# Settings > Integrations > Private Apps > Create. Scopes, the set this script
# is known to need:
#   crm.schemas.contacts.write   create the nine contact properties
#   crm.schemas.deals.write      create the deal properties, write the pipeline
#   crm.objects.deals.read       check the pipeline is empty before restaging
#   crm.objects.deals.write
#
# Treat it like a password with worse blast radius: it can rewrite the CRM
# schema, and replacing a pipeline's stages strands every deal sitting on them.
# Rotate under the app's Auth tab. Rotation kills the old token immediately, so
# update this file in the same sitting.
#
# Leave it unset and the script refuses to run rather than half-applying.
HUBSPOT_TOKEN=

# --- n8n intake pipeline --------------------------------------------------
# Where app/api/intake/route.ts fires the downstream pipeline, AFTER the raw
# payload is safely in Supabase. Capture is the route's job; normalisation,
# validation, HubSpot and notification are n8n's, per section 4 of
# purview_system_scope.md.
#
# The POST body is { row_id, payload }. row_id is the pv_intake_raw id the
# route just wrote, and n8n writes the HubSpot ids back to that row. Do not
# await it on the visitor's path: capture already succeeded, and the visitor
# should not wait on HubSpot.
#
# Leave the URL unset and nothing downstream runs. Capture is unaffected, the
# row still exists, and the pipeline can be replayed against it later.
N8N_INTAKE_WEBHOOK_URL=

# Shared secret for the X-Purview-Intake header the webhook requires.
#
# The webhook creates HubSpot contacts and deals, so an unauthenticated one is
# an open write endpoint on the CRM for anyone holding the URL. The value must
# match the n8n Header Auth credential exactly; a mismatch is a 403 and the
# pipeline silently does nothing, which the daily verification job catches as
# rows stuck at received.
#
# Rotate by changing both sides in the same sitting.
N8N_INTAKE_SECRET=
